Here, I'll explain the print and input functions, ifs and elses, and variables.

VARIABLES
Variables are kind of like pieces of information in code. If you want to store some data, then use a variable! Example:
age = 10
Now, what "age" is will be stored as data in your program.
PRINT
First of all, a print() will display text on the screen. For example:
print("Hello, world!")

That will display the text "Hello, world!" when you run the code.

INPUT
An input() will display text like a print() - but it also asks the user for input. Unlike a print, the next line of code will not run until the user enters
their input. Here's an example:
input("Hello, what is your name?")
print("That's a nice name!")

This will ask the user what their name is. The print() telling the user that they have a nice name will not run until the user enters their name.
Also, you can put input() in a variable, like this:   
ask_name = input("What is your name?")

This is helpful if you want to access the input again later, like in an if statement. We'll go over if statements in a moment.

IF & ELSE
If and Else statements are... well, if THIS is true, then THIS will happen. If not, then THIS will happen. Example:
if ask_name == "Sunny":
    print("Wow! That's my name too!")
else: 
    print("Okay! That's a nice name!")

You need to indent everything inside an if or else by 4 spaces. You also need 2 equal signs because 2 of them will check to see if the variable IS EQUAL TO
the thing you want it to be. If you are checking if the variable is equal to a phrase, you need quotes around the phrase, like in the example I showed you.
However, if you want to check if a variable is equal to ANOTHER variable, you won't need the quotes:
if your_favorites == my_favorites:
    print("Whoa! We like the same things!")


